  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Sun Jul 12 2026\n\nNot much change in the forecast to speak of as strong ridging\ncontinues to build over the Upper Midwest, leading to several \ndays of hot & humid conditions. Low & mid-level airflow \ntrajectories under the ridge will keep the worst of the heat & \nhumidity away from the area, but we're still expecting several \ndays wit highs in the 90s & heat index values of 95 to 100 \ndegrees. The heat impacts will be especially felt in the urban \ncore of the Twin Cities metro where overnight lows are only \nexpected to drop to 73-74 degrees nightly, meaning little \ncooling relief is expected overnight for those without at source\nof cooling at home. Status quo continues with the Heat Advisory\nacross central Minnesota & western Wisconsin, while an Extreme \nHeat Warning is in effect for the Twin Cities metro. These heat \nheadlines go through Tuesday night, but we'll have to evaluate \nwhether they will need to be extended later in the week. \nGuidance generally shows temepratures cooling more into the low\n90s after the heat peaks on Monday & Tuesday, which would place\nmost areas under the thresholds for excessive heat criteria. \nConfidence on the heat forecast decreases as we get to Friday &\nover the weekend, with a wide spread in forecast high \ntemperatures present among deterministic & AI guidance. Notable \nthe ECMWF deterministic still is showing potential highs over \n100 degrees this weekend (though it has been a warm outlier so \nfar) while the ECMWF AI forecast system keeps highs \"cooler\" in\nthe upper 80s to low 90s. The main takeaway from the heat \nforecast this week is the extended duration of days with \npotential excessive heat, instead of the magnitude of the heat \non a given day. While we won't be setting any daily records with\nthe heat this week, the multi-day duration of the event means \neveryone needs to be mindful of the proper heat precautions & \nensure a place to cool down.\n\nAs one would expect with an upper level ridge of this magnitude\nparked directly over the region, little chance for rain is\nexpected through the week as any systems riding along the \nperiphery of the ridge should remain well off to our north \nacross Canada & the northern Great Lakes. We could see some\nshort-term enhancement to the heat wave develop by the weekend \nas the ground dries out under day after day of baking heat & is\neasier to warm up by a few degrees daily. Our next shot for any\nsort of appreciable rain looks to come later over the weekend \nwhen the ridge begins to weaken over the region & a number of \nupper level disturbances pass over the area.",
